# Commit Practices

## Commit Structure
- Use descriptive, concise subject lines (max 72 characters)
- Structure commit messages following conventional commits format:
  - `type(scope): description` (e.g., `feat(schema): add base node schema`)
- Types include: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore, perf
- When including breaking changes, add `BREAKING CHANGE:` in the body
- Reference issues/tickets where applicable

## Pre-Commit Actions
- Run linters and formatters before committing
- Ensure all tests pass locally
- Consider running `pnpm run ci:local` to catch issues early
- Review your changes for unintended modifications

## Post-Commit Actions
- Check off completed items in the implementation plan
- Update relevant documentation to reflect changes
- Create/update checkpoint reports when reaching milestones
- Consider whether the changes warrant a PR for review
- Keep the project in a consistent state at all times

## Meaningful Commits
- Keep commits focused on single logical changes
- Break down large changes into smaller, logical commits
- Include context in commit messages to explain "why" not just "what"
- Ensure each commit maintains a working state of the codebase

## Pull Requests
- Create pull requests for significant changes
- Update PR description with checkpoint reports when applicable
- Link PRs to relevant implementation plan items
- When pushing a commit on a branch with an existing PR, update the PR description to reflect new changes

Following these practices helps maintain a clear development history and supports the stateful development approach.
